WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:09.179
Senator Bernie Sanders wants the federal
government to provide a $15-per-hour-job

00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:12.809
to every American who wants one. It's
hard to overstate how silly this

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:17.220
proposal is. Taxes could never be raised,
and existing programs could never be cut

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.060
enough to finance this policy at the
level required, and the government could

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.019
not possibly come up with enough jobs to
satisfy its obligations under this plan.

00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:28.830
This cartoonish proposal is predicated
in part on the notion that there are "bad

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.820
jobs", jobs that are beneath the dignity
of what a reasonable person should

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.510
expect from employment. It's true, of
course, that many jobs are physically

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:40.260
demanding and pay relatively little. But
it's also true that work confers dignity

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:45.840
and is virtuous. Flipping burgers for $10
an hour isn't a bad job. It's an

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.110
opportunity to build skills and climb
the employment ladder; it's an

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:53.039
opportunity to provide for yourself and
your family; it's an opportunity to make

00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:56.699
a contribution to your community and to
society. All leaders should encourage

00:00:56.699 --> 00:01:01.469
work. Leaders shouldn't denigrate – even
unintentionally – the jobs held by

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:06.640
millions of hardworking Americans. That
message is counterproductive. Messages matter.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.560
What do you think about Senator Sanders's proposal for a guaranteed

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.939
job for every American? Let us know in
your comments. Also, let us know what

00:01:14.939 --> 00:01:19.049
other topics you'd like our scholars to
cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:24.320
and subscribe for more research and
videos from AEI.

